WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.512 --> 00:00:05.970
PATRICK LORTON:
Hi, I'm Pat Lorton.

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:08.012
I'm the CTO of Schrodinger,
a company many of you

00:00:08.012 --> 00:00:09.840
may not have heard of,
working on problems

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.010
you also may not have heard of.

00:00:12.010 --> 00:00:14.063
But I'm excited to talk
to you about both today.

00:00:14.063 --> 00:00:16.230
I think there's a lot of
interesting stuff in there,

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:17.760
even for people who
aren't from the field.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:18.690
So who is Schrodinger?

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:19.490
What do you do?

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:21.240
Those are going to be
the first questions.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:22.890
We're an almost
30-year-old company

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:25.188
that is committed both
to fundamental research

00:00:25.188 --> 00:00:27.480
and applying that research
to accelerate drug discovery

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:28.748
material design.

00:00:28.748 --> 00:00:30.540
Our approach to
accelerating drug discovery

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:32.850
is to simulate chemistry
on the computer.

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:35.017
I imagine that's very
fascinating to a lot of people

00:00:35.017 --> 00:00:35.517
in here.

00:00:35.517 --> 00:00:37.017
And it allows us
to make predictions

00:00:37.017 --> 00:00:38.820
about how drugs will
work in the human body

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:40.987
without ever having to
create those drugs in the lab

00:00:40.987 --> 00:00:43.740
and test them until much
later in the process.

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:46.380
I won't have enough time today
to talk about materials design,

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:48.463
but there's also a lot of
really fascinating stuff

00:00:48.463 --> 00:00:51.353
there from OLED
design to perfumes,

00:00:51.353 --> 00:00:52.770
just tons of
fascinating chemistry

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:55.260
we're able to simulate
in those fields.

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:58.153
I will start with a quick
walk-through of the agenda.

00:00:58.153 --> 00:00:59.820
One thing I really
want to state upfront

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:02.430
is I'm trying to
present in a way,

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:05.218
realizing that most people,
this field is completely novel,

00:01:05.218 --> 00:01:06.760
and you don't know
anything about it.

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:09.570
So you don't have to know
about computational chemistry,

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:11.220
or even chemistry,
for that matter.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:12.750
I'm going to try
to break it down

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:16.230
into the core
problems of computers.

00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:18.000
But I will also
give some background

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:19.110
because otherwise it's
just not going to be

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:20.670
an interesting presentation.

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:23.310
So first, we'll start with,
why is drug discovery so

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:24.900
hard or expensive?

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:26.748
You've probably read about this.

00:01:26.748 --> 00:01:28.290
Newspapers talk
about it all the time

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:29.790
when this new drug
comes out, and it

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:32.560
costs $200,000 for a cure for
something, and everyone says,

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:33.860
how can you charge that much?

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:35.985
They'll reference that it
costs billions of dollars

00:01:35.985 --> 00:01:37.170
to discover the drug.

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:37.710
Why?

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:39.252
Why does it cost
billions of dollars?

00:01:39.252 --> 00:01:40.480
So I'll start with that.

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:42.870
And then I'll go to
computational chemistry, which

00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:45.760
is a field that is really more
than 50 years old at this point

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:49.440
but is seeing a renaissance
with modern compute.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:51.480
I'll talk about
Schrodinger's solution, which

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:53.430
uses the cutting
edge computational

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:56.550
chemistry to solve
pharmaceutical problems.

00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:59.160
And then from there,
after that background,

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:01.470
I'll go into the compute
requirements, the real meat

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:04.440
and potatoes, and
we'll figure out

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:07.950
what GCP is doing that's driving
our science and driving drug

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:10.110
discovery faster.

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:13.168
In that part, talking about how
GCP is speeding up Schrodinger,

00:02:13.168 --> 00:02:14.460
I'll go through several things.

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:15.918
I'm not going to
read him off here,

00:02:15.918 --> 00:02:18.750
but there are many ways that GCP
is really being helpful to us

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:21.240
as a company and as
the field in a whole.

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:28.040
So let's jump in with
this big, crazy number.

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:32.175
There are more than 10 to 60
potential drug-like molecules.

00:02:32.175 --> 00:02:34.550
Some estimates go all the way
up to 10 to the 100th or 10

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:35.870
to the 120th.

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:38.780
That is just an
astronomically big number.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:41.900
By some estimates, it's more
potential drug molecules

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:45.080
than there are atoms in
the entire universe, which

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:47.360
is hard to even fathom.

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:49.640
And we need to find the
best one for each disease.

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:50.990
That's a little bit strong.

00:02:50.990 --> 00:02:53.015
It doesn't have to be
the absolute best one.

00:02:53.015 --> 00:02:54.890
But we have to find a
really, really good one

00:02:54.890 --> 00:02:55.598
for each disease.

00:02:55.598 --> 00:02:57.950
And when you're looking at
that many potential drug-like

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:00.810
molecules, it's an
incredibly daunting task.

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:03.350
It's not a needle in a haystack.

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:06.800
It is not even an
atom in a haystack.

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:09.080
It's smaller than an
electron in a haystack.

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:11.180
That's how small
of a thing we're

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:15.270
looking for when we're
trying to find a good drug.

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:18.350
So why is drug discovery
as a whole so hard?

00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:20.720
I just got into nearly
infinite chemical space.

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:24.530
That's one of the most daunting
tasks in preclinical drug

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:26.840
discovery when we're
looking for the right drug,

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:30.470
before we take it into
human clinical testing.

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:32.100
Accurate evaluation's
really hard.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:33.170
Even if you make a guess.

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:36.320
If I just randomly am able to
pick the magical molecule out

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:38.900
of my head and make a perfect
guess, synthesizing that

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:41.720
in a lab costs somewhere
from hundreds to thousands

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:43.970
and, in some extreme cases,
even hundreds of thousands

00:03:43.970 --> 00:03:47.460
of dollars, to produce that
molecule so you can test it.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:51.410
So figuring out how to test
ideas faster and cheaper

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:53.150
can really bring down costs.

00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:57.260
When you're saying, oh, let's
try to do it computationally,

00:03:57.260 --> 00:03:59.940
the problems are extremely
computationally hard.

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:03.210
So it's a constant
negotiation between accuracy,

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:06.350
which we may never be
able to compute perfectly,

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:08.300
or something really,
really fast that

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:11.440
might have so low accuracy you
can't really make decisions off

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:11.940
of it.

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:16.519
So that's the pull and the
push in computational work.

00:04:16.519 --> 00:04:18.380
And then if you do
all of that right,

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:20.540
if all your preclinical
work goes perfectly,

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:22.190
biology is also really hard.

00:04:22.190 --> 00:04:23.810
Biology is where
drug design projects

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:26.450
start because you have to know
what biological target you're

00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:27.470
going after.

00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:29.630
And it's also where they
end because, when they're

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:31.430
going into the
clinic, you're really

00:04:31.430 --> 00:04:34.010
testing whether the
chemistry works well

00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:35.600
in the human biology.

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:37.280
And you had theories
going into it,

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:40.370
but your theories are
really just a hypothesis

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:43.610
until you've done large-scale
clinical trial testing.

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:46.550
And many, many-- the vast
majority of programs,

00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:48.650
actually-- fail during
clinical testing.

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:51.440
So even if you do everything
perfect chemically,

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:53.180
which is really
expensive and hard,

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:54.950
you still stand a
decent chance of failing

00:04:54.950 --> 00:04:57.440
because the fundamental
biology is wrong.

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:03.300
So that's why it's so
dang hard and expensive.

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:05.690
So what is
computational chemistry?

00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:08.528
I won't bore you by
reading the entire thing.

00:05:08.528 --> 00:05:10.070
And you probably
guessed by the name.

00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:13.370
It's using computers to
make chemistry predictions.

00:05:13.370 --> 00:05:14.840
It's an incredibly hard problem.

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:18.078
Without using numerical
analysis or clever estimations,

00:05:18.078 --> 00:05:20.120
many chemistry problems
are more complex to solve

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:21.770
than the hardest
encryption algorithms.

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:25.040
We're talking, it is easier to
crack the hardest encryption

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:29.030
algorithm than it would be to
get the exact accurate answer

00:05:29.030 --> 00:05:32.030
for a chemical question, which
is really, really daunting,

00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:34.040
so we have to try to estimate.

00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:35.780
The main goal of
this field, actually,

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:37.970
is to try to get as accurate
a solution as possible

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:40.490
with as fast of
estimations as possible.

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:44.620
Why is it so hard?

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:47.340
Well, again, we're going back
to the nearly infinite chemical

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:48.930
space.

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:51.220
The more predictions than
atoms in the universe,

00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:52.650
if you take that
to the next step

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:54.567
when you're thinking
about the infinite space,

00:05:54.567 --> 00:05:57.640
that means it is literally
impossible to store.

00:05:57.640 --> 00:06:00.502
If you somehow could
calculate the perfect value

00:06:00.502 --> 00:06:02.460
on every single one of
those, you couldn't even

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:04.918
store them because there's not
enough atoms in the universe

00:06:04.918 --> 00:06:06.190
to put that data anywhere.

00:06:06.190 --> 00:06:09.540
So you have to be really,
really clever in what you sample

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:11.340
and how you store it.

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:12.900
Accurate evaluation's
really hard.

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:14.130
What I was mentioning
a second ago

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:15.780
about how it can be more
difficult than an encryption

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:16.710
algorithm.

00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:21.150
These are NP-hard problems and
very hard NP-hard problems.

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:23.638
Quantum computers may one
day crack some of them,

00:06:23.638 --> 00:06:25.930
but there are a lot of problems
that are just not going

00:06:25.930 --> 00:06:28.260
to be amenable to
quantum computers ever

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:32.250
just because of the nature of
the data and things like that.

00:06:32.250 --> 00:06:36.180
Then you start talking
about how big--

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:38.167
If it's that hard for a
simple water molecule,

00:06:38.167 --> 00:06:39.750
like I mentioned
here-- how big it is.

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:41.500
Proteins are actually huge.

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:43.860
So if it's so hard that
a quantum computer still

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:46.560
can't solve for a water molecule
or the fastest supercomputers

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:49.830
are having problems getting
valid values for a water

00:06:49.830 --> 00:06:51.750
molecule, we're talking
hundreds of thousands

00:06:51.750 --> 00:06:54.600
of atoms, orders of
magnitude larger than water,

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:56.690
that we need to be able
to accurately predict

00:06:56.690 --> 00:06:59.100
to make a drug prediction.

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:00.780
Huge amounts of
science, 50 years,

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:04.210
have gone into accurately
estimating relatively cheaply.

00:07:04.210 --> 00:07:07.050
And then the compute
is massive and uneven,

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:09.330
so that is something
that the cloud has really

00:07:09.330 --> 00:07:11.880
helped a lot because, in certain
phases of a drug discovery

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:15.750
project, you might want
50,000 GPUs to be working

00:07:15.750 --> 00:07:18.240
for a day or two to get you
an answer quickly so you

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:20.380
can drive decisions in the lab.

00:07:20.380 --> 00:07:22.590
But then you'll have
a three-week synthesis

00:07:22.590 --> 00:07:25.413
cycle in the lab where you just
don't need those GPUs at all.

00:07:25.413 --> 00:07:27.330
And if you're at a big
pharmaceutical company,

00:07:27.330 --> 00:07:29.038
you might be able to
balance it by having

00:07:29.038 --> 00:07:30.930
so many projects
vying for that time,

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:33.750
but a huge amount of
fundamental drug research

00:07:33.750 --> 00:07:35.490
is done at smaller
biotechs that don't

00:07:35.490 --> 00:07:39.270
have that advantage of dozens
of active projects going on.

00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:42.120
So I'm going to stop from
the more compute-intensive

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:44.593
description and do a
really quick science break.

00:07:44.593 --> 00:07:46.260
I know most of you
won't know chemistry,

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:49.410
so this just looks like three
random pictures on the screen.

00:07:49.410 --> 00:07:53.130
So let's pretend that our
brains are neural networks

00:07:53.130 --> 00:07:55.500
and try to do some
pattern recognition here.

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:58.200
Solubility is one of the
most important properties

00:07:58.200 --> 00:07:59.642
in drug design.

00:07:59.642 --> 00:08:02.100
We've spent a huge amount of
resources trying to accurately

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:04.310
predict solubility.

00:08:04.310 --> 00:08:06.930
Solubility, just as
a reminder, is simply

00:08:06.930 --> 00:08:12.690
when you drop a drug in
water, will it actually

00:08:12.690 --> 00:08:13.770
dissolve into the water?

00:08:13.770 --> 00:08:16.690
If it doesn't dissolve into the
water, if it just sits there,

00:08:16.690 --> 00:08:19.110
then what you have is
extremely expensive brick dust

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:21.172
because it will just
sit in your system

00:08:21.172 --> 00:08:23.130
and go completely through
you without your body

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:25.090
ever absorbing it
and using the drug.

00:08:25.090 --> 00:08:27.150
So you have to find
something that's soluble.

00:08:27.150 --> 00:08:30.970
So looking at it from an image
recognition neural network,

00:08:30.970 --> 00:08:34.380
these are 2D representations
of three different structures.

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:40.260
So let's try to guess the
third structure based on two

00:08:40.260 --> 00:08:42.549
where we already
know the answer.

00:08:42.549 --> 00:08:46.662
So the left one has
mediocre solubility.

00:08:46.662 --> 00:08:48.870
It'll break down in water
a bit, but maybe not enough

00:08:48.870 --> 00:08:50.640
for a drug to be effective.

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:53.520
The far right one
has no solubility.

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:57.610
It'll end up as chalk in
the bottom of your water,

00:08:57.610 --> 00:09:01.680
and the middle one looks like
something between the two.

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:03.913
So it's really hard to guess.

00:09:03.913 --> 00:09:06.330
Maybe it has a little better
solubility than the left one.

00:09:06.330 --> 00:09:10.025
Maybe it has in between,
but from this data,

00:09:10.025 --> 00:09:11.400
there's just not
much you can do.

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:14.903
And while you can train
on huge, huge data sets,

00:09:14.903 --> 00:09:16.320
there are some
inherent properties

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:19.320
that make it so that it's
really, really local.

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:21.630
And there are these things
called activity cliffs

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:24.210
where changes happen very
dramatically in chemistry that

00:09:24.210 --> 00:09:26.160
make active learning
and machine learning

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:28.980
very difficult to solve
problems like this.

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:30.480
So you might have
guessed that I was

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:32.610
going to give you a surprise.

00:09:32.610 --> 00:09:36.040
This compound in the middle is
actually very, very soluble.

00:09:36.040 --> 00:09:38.510
If you drop it in water
and it comes apart,

00:09:38.510 --> 00:09:40.590
you can very effectively
deliver a drug

00:09:40.590 --> 00:09:42.700
with this type of solubility.

00:09:42.700 --> 00:09:43.885
So, why?

00:09:43.885 --> 00:09:45.010
What's the difference here?

00:09:47.660 --> 00:09:49.950
So the insoluble compound,
to understand it,

00:09:49.950 --> 00:09:51.630
you have to go to 3D.

00:09:51.630 --> 00:09:54.630
This is taking that
insoluble compound,

00:09:54.630 --> 00:09:57.210
rendering it in 3D,
like it is in real life,

00:09:57.210 --> 00:09:59.940
and then packing it into
a crystal, similar to how

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:02.940
water packs into ice crystals.

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:05.340
You can see all of
those blue connections

00:10:05.340 --> 00:10:08.340
there between the different
copies of the molecule.

00:10:08.340 --> 00:10:11.520
Those blue connections are
moderately energetically

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:12.130
favorable.

00:10:12.130 --> 00:10:13.620
So if you have a bunch
of blue connections,

00:10:13.620 --> 00:10:16.037
that might be enough to hold
something together, maybe not

00:10:16.037 --> 00:10:17.340
forever but for a long time.

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:19.080
You can also see the
yellow connections.

00:10:19.080 --> 00:10:21.750
Those are very, very
energetically favorable.

00:10:21.750 --> 00:10:23.430
What that means
is that, with all

00:10:23.430 --> 00:10:25.050
those yellow and
blue connections,

00:10:25.050 --> 00:10:27.600
this molecule really
likes to hold onto itself.

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:29.710
It packs really well together.

00:10:29.710 --> 00:10:31.590
And if it's packed
together, it's

00:10:31.590 --> 00:10:33.840
not going to be tempted to
go swimming with water when

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:34.632
water comes around.

00:10:34.632 --> 00:10:38.020
It's going to hold onto itself.

00:10:38.020 --> 00:10:40.600
If you look at the
moderately soluble molecule,

00:10:40.600 --> 00:10:43.690
you can kind of
get an idea for why

00:10:43.690 --> 00:10:45.783
it is able to come
apart a little bit.

00:10:45.783 --> 00:10:47.950
You'll see there are none
of those yellow lines that

00:10:47.950 --> 00:10:49.300
were really, really strong.

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:52.030
All you have is the
blue lines, which is

00:10:52.030 --> 00:10:53.393
a weaker type of interaction.

00:10:53.393 --> 00:10:55.060
But there are enough
of those blue lines

00:10:55.060 --> 00:10:57.680
that it still holds
together quite well.

00:10:57.680 --> 00:11:01.690
So what you see when you take
the other molecule, which

00:11:01.690 --> 00:11:05.350
we don't even have a good
crystal structure packing here,

00:11:05.350 --> 00:11:07.060
is that you can't
really pack it in a way

00:11:07.060 --> 00:11:08.740
where there's a lot of
good energetic connections.

00:11:08.740 --> 00:11:10.657
There's many different
ways you could pack it,

00:11:10.657 --> 00:11:13.720
but when it ends up that water
comes around, that there's so

00:11:13.720 --> 00:11:16.060
few interactions, and
water is able to interact

00:11:16.060 --> 00:11:18.880
with it favorably that it
just pulls it right out.

00:11:18.880 --> 00:11:21.880
This is really hard to predict
with just 2D or 1D data.

00:11:21.880 --> 00:11:23.710
And honestly, we've
run a bunch of ML

00:11:23.710 --> 00:11:26.170
because we're interested in
machine learning a lot as well.

00:11:26.170 --> 00:11:29.830
And even when you take 3D
data into consideration,

00:11:29.830 --> 00:11:32.230
if you're not actively looking
at how the molecules can

00:11:32.230 --> 00:11:34.950
twist and turn and
move with each other,

00:11:34.950 --> 00:11:40.515
the simple 3D snapshot is just
not enough to predict this.

00:11:40.515 --> 00:11:42.390
This has been your
science break, by the way.

00:11:42.390 --> 00:11:44.473
I won't try to teach you
any more chemistry today,

00:11:44.473 --> 00:11:47.280
but I figured it'd be
useful at some level

00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:51.580
to understand what type of
properties we're talking about.

00:11:51.580 --> 00:11:54.610
Now, that property,
solubility, it's pretty tough,

00:11:54.610 --> 00:11:55.810
as I was just talking about.

00:11:55.810 --> 00:11:58.690
It's actually relatively easy
because solubility, at least,

00:11:58.690 --> 00:12:00.820
is only specific
to the molecule.

00:12:00.820 --> 00:12:02.470
A lot of drug-like
properties are

00:12:02.470 --> 00:12:04.697
how that drug molecule
reacts with giant proteins

00:12:04.697 --> 00:12:07.030
or with other molecules that
are much harder to predict.

00:12:07.030 --> 00:12:09.010
At least solubility, you're
only thinking about that one

00:12:09.010 --> 00:12:10.540
molecule it's interacting with.

00:12:10.540 --> 00:12:13.510
But it's also only one
of many, many properties

00:12:13.510 --> 00:12:15.970
we have to optimize, and
all of those properties

00:12:15.970 --> 00:12:17.260
have to be good.

00:12:17.260 --> 00:12:20.800
If any of those properties
are bad, it will kill a drug.

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:25.030
And more evil than that is that,
not only are these properties

00:12:25.030 --> 00:12:27.520
typically not correlated
at all, but some of them

00:12:27.520 --> 00:12:28.880
are even anticorrelated.

00:12:28.880 --> 00:12:30.640
So when you improve
a property, it's

00:12:30.640 --> 00:12:33.020
guaranteed to make
another property worse.

00:12:33.020 --> 00:12:35.650
So you're trying to find
this tightrope between two

00:12:35.650 --> 00:12:37.900
properties where you can get
it right inside the range

00:12:37.900 --> 00:12:39.820
of acceptability.

00:12:39.820 --> 00:12:42.470
This is long,
laborious, tedious work,

00:12:42.470 --> 00:12:44.740
and when every guess you
have about an improvement

00:12:44.740 --> 00:12:48.640
on the molecule requires weeks
or months and tens of thousands

00:12:48.640 --> 00:12:52.410
of dollars of synthesis,
it quickly adds up.

00:12:52.410 --> 00:12:54.910
You can start to understand,
with the vastness of molecules,

00:12:54.910 --> 00:12:57.010
how hard this guesswork
is and how expensive it

00:12:57.010 --> 00:12:58.180
is to try a theory.

00:12:58.180 --> 00:13:03.450
You can start to understand
why drug discovery is so hard.

00:13:03.450 --> 00:13:06.840
I guess that's probably really
the end of the science lesson

00:13:06.840 --> 00:13:11.080
here, but it is going to go
to compute soon, I promise.

00:13:11.080 --> 00:13:13.000
So how is Schrodinger
solving this?

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:15.948
So we have a massive investment
in fundamental research.

00:13:15.948 --> 00:13:17.490
That's something
we've really focused

00:13:17.490 --> 00:13:19.600
on going all the way back.

00:13:19.600 --> 00:13:21.240
We were founded by
Rich Friesner, who's

00:13:21.240 --> 00:13:23.290
still at Columbia, in
the chemistry department

00:13:23.290 --> 00:13:27.067
at Columbia, a professor.

00:13:27.067 --> 00:13:28.900
And going all the way
back to the beginning,

00:13:28.900 --> 00:13:30.580
we've really focused
on scientists.

00:13:30.580 --> 00:13:32.110
We have over 100--

00:13:32.110 --> 00:13:34.270
I think, actually, it's
over 150 PhD scientists

00:13:34.270 --> 00:13:37.390
at this point who all they
do is try to figure out

00:13:37.390 --> 00:13:39.850
how these fundamental physics
work on a chemical level

00:13:39.850 --> 00:13:43.510
and how we can encode
that in our algorithms.

00:13:43.510 --> 00:13:46.150
The next step from the science
is finding that perfect balance

00:13:46.150 --> 00:13:47.230
of accuracy and speed.

00:13:47.230 --> 00:13:49.360
And this is something
both our scientists

00:13:49.360 --> 00:13:53.350
and a large engineering
force work very regularly on.

00:13:53.350 --> 00:13:56.080
Sometimes that's as simple
as speeding up our code.

00:13:56.080 --> 00:13:58.690
Sometimes, it's moving
things onto the GPU.

00:13:58.690 --> 00:14:01.660
But sometimes, it's stepping
back and saying, on a chemical

00:14:01.660 --> 00:14:05.920
level, what things can we take
for granted that will allow

00:14:05.920 --> 00:14:07.930
us to simplify the algorithm?

00:14:07.930 --> 00:14:10.460
It wouldn't be robust to
everything in the world,

00:14:10.460 --> 00:14:12.950
but for this problem,
what can we simplify?

00:14:12.950 --> 00:14:15.880
So we do a lot of that.

00:14:15.880 --> 00:14:18.315
We do, luckily, scale
embarrassingly parallel.

00:14:18.315 --> 00:14:19.690
Almost all of
these calculations,

00:14:19.690 --> 00:14:21.670
since you're looking
at one drug at a time

00:14:21.670 --> 00:14:24.190
and you might want to look at
batches of 10,000, that allows

00:14:24.190 --> 00:14:27.010
you to scale up really, really
quickly as long as you have

00:14:27.010 --> 00:14:28.285
the infrastructure to do so.

00:14:28.285 --> 00:14:30.160
But the last one then
comes back around which

00:14:30.160 --> 00:14:32.110
is, if you're
calculating information

00:14:32.110 --> 00:14:33.728
on billions or tens
of billions or,

00:14:33.728 --> 00:14:35.770
in the future, hundreds
of billions and trillions

00:14:35.770 --> 00:14:37.150
of compounds, how
do you actually

00:14:37.150 --> 00:14:39.070
deal with all that
data you're generating?

00:14:39.070 --> 00:14:41.320
And that's a big
problem for us as well.

00:14:41.320 --> 00:14:45.397
And how that works is,
on the very left phase,

00:14:45.397 --> 00:14:46.480
we're looking at billions.

00:14:46.480 --> 00:14:48.480
I think you can actually
go up to 10 to the 10th

00:14:48.480 --> 00:14:50.060
at this point where
we're calculating

00:14:50.060 --> 00:14:54.280
very fast properties on CPUs
for billions of compounds.

00:14:54.280 --> 00:14:58.510
We use some machine learning
and some other simple algorithms

00:14:58.510 --> 00:15:00.582
to get that down to
a small enough number

00:15:00.582 --> 00:15:02.290
that we want to push
forward, and then we

00:15:02.290 --> 00:15:04.780
start these cycles where
we improve the molecule,

00:15:04.780 --> 00:15:07.995
we make guesses, we improve
our active learning.

00:15:07.995 --> 00:15:09.370
Humans will evaluate
the designs,

00:15:09.370 --> 00:15:12.010
humans will synthesize an
assay, and we'll slowly

00:15:12.010 --> 00:15:14.230
be improving these properties.

00:15:14.230 --> 00:15:17.685
The goal is to do this cycle
as few times as possible.

00:15:17.685 --> 00:15:19.060
If you only had
to do it once, it

00:15:19.060 --> 00:15:23.050
would be this dream world
where you could go from an idea

00:15:23.050 --> 00:15:28.120
to a drug candidate going
into the clinic within weeks

00:15:28.120 --> 00:15:29.470
or a few months.

00:15:29.470 --> 00:15:31.420
That's the future dream.

00:15:31.420 --> 00:15:33.282
Right now, even in the
best case scenario,

00:15:33.282 --> 00:15:34.990
you're still doing
this cycle many times.

00:15:37.880 --> 00:15:41.310
So those computing requirements
that I was referencing earlier.

00:15:41.310 --> 00:15:42.690
What do those look like?

00:15:42.690 --> 00:15:46.743
Well, we have massive
scale for CPU.

00:15:46.743 --> 00:15:48.410
Maybe it's not massive
scale for Google,

00:15:48.410 --> 00:15:51.610
but for most of us out
there, 500,000 CPUs

00:15:51.610 --> 00:15:54.270
is a lot of CPUs to
be using at once.

00:15:54.270 --> 00:15:57.710
That's more than a thousand to
a person working at our company,

00:15:57.710 --> 00:16:00.890
to give you an
idea of that scale.

00:16:00.890 --> 00:16:03.050
We also regularly
need a diverse GPU.

00:16:03.050 --> 00:16:05.210
So the GPUs are
actually the workhorses

00:16:05.210 --> 00:16:07.100
for our most important
calculations.

00:16:07.100 --> 00:16:12.020
We have gotten relatively far
down the filter of accuracy

00:16:12.020 --> 00:16:14.670
by the time we get to
the GPU calculations.

00:16:14.670 --> 00:16:16.490
So we're doing a
lot fewer molecules,

00:16:16.490 --> 00:16:18.890
which allows us to only maybe
have 10,000 things running

00:16:18.890 --> 00:16:21.500
at once instead of 500,000
things running at once.

00:16:21.500 --> 00:16:24.560
That said, it's
still a lot of work.

00:16:24.560 --> 00:16:27.570
We are lucky that it's
relatively small input,

00:16:27.570 --> 00:16:30.920
and the final output is still
relatively small in terms

00:16:30.920 --> 00:16:32.930
of its terabytes.

00:16:32.930 --> 00:16:36.440
If you were to keep track of
all the data we generate along

00:16:36.440 --> 00:16:38.350
the way for those
predictions, it's

00:16:38.350 --> 00:16:40.820
into the many, many
petabytes of data.

00:16:40.820 --> 00:16:42.620
But luckily, we only
really need to save

00:16:42.620 --> 00:16:45.020
some snapshots or
final snapshots

00:16:45.020 --> 00:16:46.130
of these trajectories.

00:16:46.130 --> 00:16:48.630
We don't have to keep
all the data together.

00:16:48.630 --> 00:16:50.450
So relatively small
end on the order

00:16:50.450 --> 00:16:54.020
of hundreds of megabytes
or gigabytes and then only

00:16:54.020 --> 00:16:55.050
maybe terabytes.

00:16:55.050 --> 00:16:58.650
So in the big sense of
the cloud, pretty small.

00:16:58.650 --> 00:17:01.190
It's rich, dense data.

00:17:01.190 --> 00:17:03.690
It's that small because we want
to analyze about everything.

00:17:03.690 --> 00:17:05.720
It's not like the first step is
going and cleaning a bunch out.

00:17:05.720 --> 00:17:07.595
We really do want to
look through pretty much

00:17:07.595 --> 00:17:09.270
all of that data.

00:17:09.270 --> 00:17:11.960
And then, finally, how are we
actually calculating things?

00:17:11.960 --> 00:17:13.460
We have very different
requirements.

00:17:13.460 --> 00:17:15.835
Sometimes, we need a GPU node
with only 12 gigs of memory

00:17:15.835 --> 00:17:16.880
or even less, actually.

00:17:16.880 --> 00:17:19.160
I think we have some
six gig instances.

00:17:19.160 --> 00:17:21.500
And sometimes, we need
400 gigs on the GPU node,

00:17:21.500 --> 00:17:26.238
so the ability that GCP has to
allow you to dynamically pick

00:17:26.238 --> 00:17:28.280
your node size is really,
really powerful for us.

00:17:31.070 --> 00:17:34.300
So here is an example of a
high-memory T4 GPU nodes.

00:17:34.300 --> 00:17:37.280
So this is something that we
used to have to build in-house.

00:17:37.280 --> 00:17:39.280
We actually originally
deployed this application

00:17:39.280 --> 00:17:41.260
on an in-house server
because you simply

00:17:41.260 --> 00:17:44.350
couldn't get something
with a few GPUs and 400

00:17:44.350 --> 00:17:45.760
gigs of memory.

00:17:45.760 --> 00:17:50.855
So I will go ahead
and play this video.

00:17:55.230 --> 00:17:59.150
So what you're seeing here is
we've loaded about one and 1/2

00:17:59.150 --> 00:18:02.490
billion compounds into the
GPU's memory as a subset.

00:18:02.490 --> 00:18:04.370
We have the secondary
screening on the CPU

00:18:04.370 --> 00:18:07.160
that allows us to keep a
relatively easy microservice,

00:18:07.160 --> 00:18:09.530
if you will, that is
looking over a billion

00:18:09.530 --> 00:18:11.200
synthesizable compounds.

00:18:11.200 --> 00:18:12.973
And what we're doing
is we're saying,

00:18:12.973 --> 00:18:15.140
those compounds on the right
are searching libraries

00:18:15.140 --> 00:18:16.580
that you can just purchase
them off the shelf.

00:18:16.580 --> 00:18:18.200
You don't have to
synthesize them,

00:18:18.200 --> 00:18:20.158
which is a huge advantage
in early drug design.

00:18:20.158 --> 00:18:22.950
And we're saying, OK, I'm
drawing my IDs on the left.

00:18:22.950 --> 00:18:25.070
Find the closest thing
I can just purchase off

00:18:25.070 --> 00:18:28.735
the shelf so we can guess
and experiment with our ideas

00:18:28.735 --> 00:18:30.860
without having to invest
in custom synthesis, which

00:18:30.860 --> 00:18:32.136
is very expensive.

00:18:40.580 --> 00:18:44.050
So how are our strategies for
executing the big back end?

00:18:44.050 --> 00:18:47.560
Not that microservice, but when
we're calculating properties

00:18:47.560 --> 00:18:50.080
for these billions
down to thousands

00:18:50.080 --> 00:18:54.640
of really expensive
calculations,

00:18:54.640 --> 00:18:55.940
how do we handle that?

00:18:55.940 --> 00:18:58.660
So first, we have the
hardest that we've done.

00:18:58.660 --> 00:19:01.270
We have a team of really
good solution architects

00:19:01.270 --> 00:19:04.010
and engineers that have
built a direct cloud API.

00:19:04.010 --> 00:19:08.420
So that's how we've scaled up to
500,000 for a really long time.

00:19:08.420 --> 00:19:10.180
We are developing
the technologies

00:19:10.180 --> 00:19:13.120
to not have to use the
drug cloud API explicitly,

00:19:13.120 --> 00:19:16.680
but for a lot of our massive
scale, we're still using that.

00:19:16.680 --> 00:19:19.900
What we're moving to right
now is an auto-scaling queue

00:19:19.900 --> 00:19:21.207
system.

00:19:21.207 --> 00:19:22.540
I shouldn't say we're moving to.

00:19:22.540 --> 00:19:25.870
As much stuff as we can
handle there, we will.

00:19:25.870 --> 00:19:27.940
Slurm is our preferred
queuing system,

00:19:27.940 --> 00:19:30.700
although, we support many.

00:19:30.700 --> 00:19:32.410
It has some limitations
in somewhere

00:19:32.410 --> 00:19:36.190
on the order of a couple
hundred thousand compute nodes.

00:19:36.190 --> 00:19:38.920
But reaching that and
taking advantage of it,

00:19:38.920 --> 00:19:43.540
there's a bunch of other legacy
stuff that you're reliant on.

00:19:43.540 --> 00:19:47.062
That said, it's extremely
easy for some of our older

00:19:47.062 --> 00:19:48.520
applications to
run on because they

00:19:48.520 --> 00:19:51.307
were built to run on clusters
before the cloud existed.

00:19:51.307 --> 00:19:53.140
The future that we're
building towards right

00:19:53.140 --> 00:19:58.810
now is using Kubernetes,
and GKE specifically.

00:19:58.810 --> 00:20:01.640
On the next slide, I'll go into
the architecture details there.

00:20:01.640 --> 00:20:04.600
But we would really like
to see a world where

00:20:04.600 --> 00:20:07.330
we can think about
things in GKE clusters

00:20:07.330 --> 00:20:10.510
instead of having to
manually do cloud API.

00:20:10.510 --> 00:20:13.200
And we're hoping GKE
will evolve to the place

00:20:13.200 --> 00:20:14.950
where it can even
displace queuing systems

00:20:14.950 --> 00:20:16.750
for a lot of those tasks.

00:20:16.750 --> 00:20:20.950
The GKE setup that I
discussed a second ago--

00:20:20.950 --> 00:20:23.560
so the way we're typically
doing that now is we're

00:20:23.560 --> 00:20:24.760
using a Postgres server.

00:20:24.760 --> 00:20:26.950
Now I should mention
that, when we

00:20:26.950 --> 00:20:28.840
get to the point of
really crazy compute,

00:20:28.840 --> 00:20:30.673
we'll almost certainly
replace that Postgres

00:20:30.673 --> 00:20:32.560
server with BigQuery.

00:20:32.560 --> 00:20:34.750
Right now, it's
just easy to deploy.

00:20:34.750 --> 00:20:37.090
It also allows us to
deploy it in-house

00:20:37.090 --> 00:20:42.270
if a customer were to have
in-house requirements.

00:20:42.270 --> 00:20:45.640
The Postgres server and
the Kubernetes spinner

00:20:45.640 --> 00:20:47.860
bring up nodes, and they
hit the Kubernetes API

00:20:47.860 --> 00:20:49.300
server to bring up nodes.

00:20:49.300 --> 00:20:51.642
We scale up to as many--

00:20:51.642 --> 00:20:55.490
I forget the Kubernetes term.

00:20:55.490 --> 00:21:00.390
We scale go to as many
Kubernetes nodes as we can.

00:21:00.390 --> 00:21:03.000
And then, as that
work is finished,

00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:05.570
it'll give status back
to the Postgres server,

00:21:05.570 --> 00:21:08.040
maybe some very small number
of calculation numbers.

00:21:08.040 --> 00:21:11.340
And it'll deposit the
files into buckets.

00:21:11.340 --> 00:21:13.820
So the buckets will be used
for both input and output,

00:21:13.820 --> 00:21:15.530
highly scalable data storage.

00:21:15.530 --> 00:21:18.860
That's actually one really big
advantage over traditional HPC,

00:21:18.860 --> 00:21:21.350
which frequently still
relies on things like NFS,

00:21:21.350 --> 00:21:25.100
is the buckets are just really
high-quality scalable data

00:21:25.100 --> 00:21:26.760
storage, and cheap.

00:21:26.760 --> 00:21:29.390
So I wanted to stop a second
and say, what exactly are

00:21:29.390 --> 00:21:30.110
those GPUs doing?

00:21:30.110 --> 00:21:31.808
We're using 10,000 GPUs.

00:21:31.808 --> 00:21:33.350
I've talked about
physics simulation,

00:21:33.350 --> 00:21:34.787
but what does that look like?

00:21:34.787 --> 00:21:36.370
Also, I just really
love these videos.

00:21:36.370 --> 00:21:38.100
I think a lot of people
think they look cool,

00:21:38.100 --> 00:21:39.590
so I figured I'd
show one of these.

00:21:43.403 --> 00:21:44.070
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:21:44.070 --> 00:21:47.580
So what this is right here is
it's a free energy perturbation

00:21:47.580 --> 00:21:48.150
calculation.

00:21:48.150 --> 00:21:50.470
I won't explain exactly
what that means,

00:21:50.470 --> 00:21:52.395
but basically, that's
a drug molecule

00:21:52.395 --> 00:21:54.570
that you're seeing with
waters hovering around it

00:21:54.570 --> 00:21:56.112
inside a human protein.

00:21:56.112 --> 00:21:57.570
And what we're
trying to figure out

00:21:57.570 --> 00:21:59.580
is if we take a drug
molecule where we already

00:21:59.580 --> 00:22:03.420
know its efficacy, and we make
a small perturbation to it,

00:22:03.420 --> 00:22:07.290
will it be more effective
as a drug molecule?

00:22:07.290 --> 00:22:10.760
Will it bind better
to the protein?

00:22:10.760 --> 00:22:13.660
This calculation
requires-- here it

00:22:13.660 --> 00:22:15.220
says eight hours of GPU time.

00:22:15.220 --> 00:22:16.290
I think the video's
a few hours old,

00:22:16.290 --> 00:22:18.873
and we have it down to something
like two to four hours of GPU

00:22:18.873 --> 00:22:23.320
time, thanks a lot to NVIDIA
for speeding up their GPUs.

00:22:23.320 --> 00:22:25.840
But it takes hours on a
GPU to do this simulation

00:22:25.840 --> 00:22:27.237
for a single ligand.

00:22:27.237 --> 00:22:27.820
[END PLAYBACK]

00:22:27.820 --> 00:22:31.070
And again, go back to that
10 to the 60th drug space.

00:22:31.070 --> 00:22:34.810
You can see how infinite the
compute would be to calculate

00:22:34.810 --> 00:22:37.680
this for all available ligands.

00:22:37.680 --> 00:22:42.240
So simulating just that simple
motion, those motion mechanics,

00:22:42.240 --> 00:22:44.700
is quite expensive,
and we do what we

00:22:44.700 --> 00:22:46.050
can to reduce it when we can.

00:22:46.050 --> 00:22:49.380
So that's how we keep one
GPU busy for that one.

00:22:49.380 --> 00:22:52.320
But coming up with ideas, how
do we come up with so many ideas

00:22:52.320 --> 00:22:55.020
to keep all those GPUs busy?

00:22:55.020 --> 00:22:58.750
Well, it might kind of look like
motion because it's so blurred,

00:22:58.750 --> 00:23:02.250
but what this actually is it's
thousands and thousands of drug

00:23:02.250 --> 00:23:05.280
molecules superpositioned
on top of each other,

00:23:05.280 --> 00:23:07.380
preserving the same
core in the bottom.

00:23:07.380 --> 00:23:10.170
So those are all tiny,
tiny changes up top.

00:23:10.170 --> 00:23:13.740
And we've enumerated many,
many potential drug molecules

00:23:13.740 --> 00:23:14.982
that we think might be good.

00:23:14.982 --> 00:23:16.440
We've taken a lot
of the human work

00:23:16.440 --> 00:23:19.530
out of individually drawing
them by enumerating a bunch

00:23:19.530 --> 00:23:21.330
of different combinations.

00:23:21.330 --> 00:23:22.990
And once we've done
that, now we say,

00:23:22.990 --> 00:23:25.410
OK, we have this
big group of ones.

00:23:25.410 --> 00:23:28.740
Let's run some subset
of these through

00:23:28.740 --> 00:23:30.380
the physics-based
engine on the GPU.

00:23:30.380 --> 00:23:33.090
So say we've enumerated a
billion like I was referencing.

00:23:33.090 --> 00:23:34.410
Let's filter it down.

00:23:34.410 --> 00:23:38.080
Let's run a thousand through
GPUs for several hours,

00:23:38.080 --> 00:23:40.840
maybe 10,000 for GPUs
for several hours.

00:23:40.840 --> 00:23:42.450
And then when we
get that data back,

00:23:42.450 --> 00:23:44.263
let's train an active
learning algorithm.

00:23:44.263 --> 00:23:45.930
That active learning
algorithm will then

00:23:45.930 --> 00:23:50.100
take a guess at another thousand
values and just sporadically

00:23:50.100 --> 00:23:51.960
pick points that are
either good or bad

00:23:51.960 --> 00:23:54.030
or maybe just interesting
for other ideas

00:23:54.030 --> 00:23:55.870
and run the physics
calculation on those.

00:23:55.870 --> 00:23:57.990
And that's how we
extend the applicability

00:23:57.990 --> 00:23:59.880
domain of active learning.

00:23:59.880 --> 00:24:02.010
Active learning is
really good in this space

00:24:02.010 --> 00:24:03.180
for interpolation.

00:24:03.180 --> 00:24:05.800
When you have two things
that are just clearly

00:24:05.800 --> 00:24:07.800
two steps apart from each
other, active learning

00:24:07.800 --> 00:24:10.850
is pretty good at finding
the point in between them.

00:24:10.850 --> 00:24:12.600
It's not perfect because
sometimes there's

00:24:12.600 --> 00:24:14.040
surprise physics.

00:24:14.040 --> 00:24:16.330
We still always double-check
active learning,

00:24:16.330 --> 00:24:18.210
but it's really good.

00:24:18.210 --> 00:24:21.570
The thing that active learning
isn't great at in this space

00:24:21.570 --> 00:24:23.430
is finding truly novel things.

00:24:23.430 --> 00:24:25.802
When you come up with new
chemistry that is just

00:24:25.802 --> 00:24:28.260
going to totally change the
way the physics and the protein

00:24:28.260 --> 00:24:30.570
works, active
learning just can't

00:24:30.570 --> 00:24:33.690
guess that because it's never
seen that physics in existence

00:24:33.690 --> 00:24:34.860
before with the protein.

00:24:34.860 --> 00:24:36.810
So that's where, for
extrapolation, you really

00:24:36.810 --> 00:24:38.610
have to constantly
be running more

00:24:38.610 --> 00:24:39.960
physics-based calculations.

00:24:48.180 --> 00:24:50.880
So once we've generated
all that data,

00:24:50.880 --> 00:24:53.182
what do we actually do with it?

00:24:53.182 --> 00:24:55.640
Let's step back to the beginning
of drug discovery project,

00:24:55.640 --> 00:24:57.210
and a biologist has
discovered a protein

00:24:57.210 --> 00:24:59.430
that they're pretty sure is
strongly related to a disease.

00:24:59.430 --> 00:25:01.050
So they've done some
lab experiments,

00:25:01.050 --> 00:25:03.000
and if they knock
out that protein,

00:25:03.000 --> 00:25:08.280
the disease goes away in the
lab, not in a human being,

00:25:08.280 --> 00:25:09.653
maybe in a mouse.

00:25:09.653 --> 00:25:11.070
So let's kick off
a project trying

00:25:11.070 --> 00:25:14.430
to find molecules that could
knock out that protein.

00:25:14.430 --> 00:25:17.665
And maybe those molecules
could end up being drugs.

00:25:17.665 --> 00:25:18.790
How do we actually do that?

00:25:18.790 --> 00:25:20.420
How much data's involved?

00:25:20.420 --> 00:25:22.980
And that's what this
slide's talking about here.

00:25:22.980 --> 00:25:24.870
We probably want to
start with something

00:25:24.870 --> 00:25:27.330
on the order of 10
billion new ideas.

00:25:27.330 --> 00:25:29.862
We want to do either
high-throughput screening

00:25:29.862 --> 00:25:32.070
in the lab where they have
some special machines that

00:25:32.070 --> 00:25:33.960
spin a bunch of
fragments through, allow

00:25:33.960 --> 00:25:36.780
you to get some really
inaccurate coarse data just

00:25:36.780 --> 00:25:37.980
to start off of.

00:25:37.980 --> 00:25:40.170
Or if you're pretty
confident with what you have

00:25:40.170 --> 00:25:42.990
on the computer-- it's called
an X-ray crystal structure,

00:25:42.990 --> 00:25:44.520
3D data of the protein--

00:25:44.520 --> 00:25:48.540
you can start to do
virtual screening

00:25:48.540 --> 00:25:50.490
with just the computer
itself, without having

00:25:50.490 --> 00:25:51.225
to go to the lab.

00:25:51.225 --> 00:25:53.100
People go back and forth
on these two things.

00:25:53.100 --> 00:25:55.142
They're both fairly
inaccurate, but they find you

00:25:55.142 --> 00:25:56.940
the starting places
you need to proceed.

00:25:56.940 --> 00:25:59.550
But the problem is, say, you've
calculated these 20 properties

00:25:59.550 --> 00:26:03.292
for 10 billion things, what
do you actually do with this?

00:26:03.292 --> 00:26:05.250
Keep in mind, this is
not a computer scientist.

00:26:05.250 --> 00:26:06.958
They're usually not
even data scientists.

00:26:06.958 --> 00:26:09.990
This is just a chemist
that has picked up

00:26:09.990 --> 00:26:12.840
computers over the
years as they've had to,

00:26:12.840 --> 00:26:16.590
and we're talking about them
having to deal with parsing up

00:26:16.590 --> 00:26:19.480
to trillions of data points.

00:26:19.480 --> 00:26:22.740
So this is where GCP's
BigQuery has really

00:26:22.740 --> 00:26:24.120
been a game changer for us.

00:26:24.120 --> 00:26:27.060
We're able to set it up that
our back ends can deposit

00:26:27.060 --> 00:26:29.610
the most important data
directly into BigQuery,

00:26:29.610 --> 00:26:32.910
and people who aren't
necessarily data scientists

00:26:32.910 --> 00:26:35.070
are able to interact
with that data.

00:26:35.070 --> 00:26:38.800
So BigQuery has given us
a lot of great things.

00:26:38.800 --> 00:26:42.870
So in this example that
you're seeing, we have loaded,

00:26:42.870 --> 00:26:46.170
very easily, 1.2
billion molecules--

00:26:46.170 --> 00:26:48.510
you can see the
number of rows here--

00:26:48.510 --> 00:26:50.960
and a bunch of data
about it into BigQuery.

00:26:50.960 --> 00:26:53.460
Now normally, if we were doing
this, these terabytes of data

00:26:53.460 --> 00:26:56.490
would require a
nontrivial IT task.

00:26:56.490 --> 00:26:58.480
We'd have to set up a
database for the user.

00:26:58.480 --> 00:27:00.772
These would have to figure
out how to interact with it.

00:27:00.772 --> 00:27:03.510
We'd have to pick a UI that the
user could easily figure out,

00:27:03.510 --> 00:27:05.970
and it wouldn't scale very
well unless we really were

00:27:05.970 --> 00:27:07.490
committed to making this great.

00:27:07.490 --> 00:27:09.580
But with BigQuery,
as you just saw,

00:27:09.580 --> 00:27:13.290
you can execute this really
simple query, this basic five

00:27:13.290 --> 00:27:16.650
drug-like properties
query, and it

00:27:16.650 --> 00:27:19.360
will happen almost immediately
in just a few seconds.

00:27:19.360 --> 00:27:21.480
So the scalability
is like, we care

00:27:21.480 --> 00:27:23.550
more about the output
of this than anything

00:27:23.550 --> 00:27:27.330
else we could care about
from a database perspective.

00:27:27.330 --> 00:27:29.520
And it doesn't require
very much for the chemist

00:27:29.520 --> 00:27:31.020
to get going on it,
so it really has

00:27:31.020 --> 00:27:34.590
allowed us to think about data
in a totally different way.

00:27:34.590 --> 00:27:37.230
This example shows how
complex we can get.

00:27:37.230 --> 00:27:42.150
The first one was very simple,
but this example shows us the--

00:27:42.150 --> 00:27:45.930
again, with 1.2 billion, we've
created this really complex

00:27:45.930 --> 00:27:46.480
query.

00:27:46.480 --> 00:27:47.897
And what we're
really trying to do

00:27:47.897 --> 00:27:49.960
is sort things into
different classes.

00:27:49.960 --> 00:27:52.380
So we've loaded 1.2
billion new molecules

00:27:52.380 --> 00:27:54.120
in with all of their
data, and now we

00:27:54.120 --> 00:27:56.953
have this query that
allows us to sort those

00:27:56.953 --> 00:27:57.870
into different things.

00:27:57.870 --> 00:28:02.340
So you can see here,
in just 5.2 seconds,

00:28:02.340 --> 00:28:04.560
we've sorted 1.2
billion molecules

00:28:04.560 --> 00:28:06.240
into different
classes of molecules,

00:28:06.240 --> 00:28:09.030
whether they're their
drug-like or fragments

00:28:09.030 --> 00:28:10.590
or things like that.

00:28:10.590 --> 00:28:12.780
The ability to do
that essentially

00:28:12.780 --> 00:28:14.910
immediately is just an
absolute game changer

00:28:14.910 --> 00:28:16.050
for our data scientists.

00:28:19.710 --> 00:28:24.140
So the last really
big GCP feature for us

00:28:24.140 --> 00:28:28.070
is not nearly as fashionable as
machine learning or large scale

00:28:28.070 --> 00:28:30.950
HPC or even big data.

00:28:30.950 --> 00:28:33.620
Honestly, it matters a
lot for us, especially

00:28:33.620 --> 00:28:38.300
from the management IT
side, security side.

00:28:38.300 --> 00:28:41.600
We just love the
project layout of GCP.

00:28:41.600 --> 00:28:44.190
The isolation is
very, very natural.

00:28:44.190 --> 00:28:46.610
The user access
management is great.

00:28:46.610 --> 00:28:49.360
It gives you a lot of options,
sometimes, too many options,

00:28:49.360 --> 00:28:51.565
but it's better to have
too many than too few.

00:28:51.565 --> 00:28:53.690
And especially, if you're
a corporate G Suite user,

00:28:53.690 --> 00:28:59.330
it's just trivial to move
people into a GCP-type cloud

00:28:59.330 --> 00:29:01.130
interface.

00:29:01.130 --> 00:29:03.470
I personally find the
interface wonderful,

00:29:03.470 --> 00:29:05.738
both the web UI and
the command line tools.

00:29:05.738 --> 00:29:08.280
I don't get enough time to do
purely technical stuff anymore,

00:29:08.280 --> 00:29:10.113
so if the interface is
really easy to learn,

00:29:10.113 --> 00:29:11.760
I'm much more likely
to use the tool.

00:29:11.760 --> 00:29:14.503
And I find both interfaces
to be really, really strong.

00:29:14.503 --> 00:29:17.170
I'm not going to give a bunch of
examples here of the interface.

00:29:17.170 --> 00:29:19.190
People can play with it
themselves, but I did

00:29:19.190 --> 00:29:20.270
want to highlight
that because that

00:29:20.270 --> 00:29:22.460
was a really big part of
the reason why we chose

00:29:22.460 --> 00:29:25.200
GCP, just the ease of use.

00:29:25.200 --> 00:29:28.745
It's enjoyable both for naive
users and the DevOps experts.

00:29:33.340 --> 00:29:36.520
So to give the summary
here, Schrodinger's

00:29:36.520 --> 00:29:39.900
computational chemistry platform
can speed up drug discovery,

00:29:39.900 --> 00:29:41.560
and sometimes that's
actually directly

00:29:41.560 --> 00:29:43.420
correlated to
computational throughput.

00:29:43.420 --> 00:29:45.800
Sometimes, it's blocked by
lab work and things like that,

00:29:45.800 --> 00:29:49.155
but there are significant
parts of a project runtime

00:29:49.155 --> 00:29:50.530
where, if I just
have the ability

00:29:50.530 --> 00:29:52.900
to run more
computations at once,

00:29:52.900 --> 00:29:55.840
I will speed up how fast
the drug goes to market.

00:29:55.840 --> 00:29:57.340
And it can literally
be day for day.

00:29:57.340 --> 00:29:59.340
So if something that used
to take 10 days and it

00:29:59.340 --> 00:30:00.940
takes two days,
that means you get

00:30:00.940 --> 00:30:02.920
to that clinical stage
eight days faster,

00:30:02.920 --> 00:30:05.810
and it's eight days faster
that drug gets to market.

00:30:05.810 --> 00:30:07.510
It's pretty amazing
when you think

00:30:07.510 --> 00:30:09.490
about being able to
speed up tasks like that,

00:30:09.490 --> 00:30:12.320
and the cloud has helped a lot.

00:30:12.320 --> 00:30:14.710
GCP, specifically for all
the reasons I've listed,

00:30:14.710 --> 00:30:16.620
makes our discovery
efforts faster.

00:30:16.620 --> 00:30:19.030
It makes it more flexible,
and that flexibility directly

00:30:19.030 --> 00:30:22.390
translates into getting
things done faster.

00:30:22.390 --> 00:30:26.660
BigQuery allows our scientists
to store and analyze data.

00:30:26.660 --> 00:30:28.963
It's funny we're talking
about it in the same sentence

00:30:28.963 --> 00:30:30.880
because it seems like a
totally separate tool,

00:30:30.880 --> 00:30:33.280
but the way it integrates so
well with everything GCP's

00:30:33.280 --> 00:30:36.700
doing, and how we can reach it
from all of our compute nodes

00:30:36.700 --> 00:30:38.900
trivially-- it has great
access controls and all

00:30:38.900 --> 00:30:41.590
of that-- that integrated
nature of BigQuery

00:30:41.590 --> 00:30:44.020
really has been a game
changer when we're analyzing

00:30:44.020 --> 00:30:45.460
the big data we generate.

00:30:45.460 --> 00:30:49.240
So all in summation,
these tools come together

00:30:49.240 --> 00:30:52.330
to make GCP just a complete
solution for Schrodinger that

00:30:52.330 --> 00:30:56.460
sped us up, and we've
really invested in.

00:30:56.460 --> 00:31:00.575
And then on that note, I'm
going to go to the cool video.

00:31:00.575 --> 00:31:01.450
I love to show these.

00:31:01.450 --> 00:31:03.910
Again, I mentioned earlier,
I think people just

00:31:03.910 --> 00:31:06.820
like to think about how
molecules work inside your body

00:31:06.820 --> 00:31:08.440
even if you're not a scientist.

00:31:08.440 --> 00:31:11.320
And we've done a pretty good job
of making these things pretty.

00:31:11.320 --> 00:31:13.403
So here's a video that's
a few years old that I'll

00:31:13.403 --> 00:31:14.760
end the presentation on.

00:31:18.077 --> 00:31:18.744
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:31:18.744 --> 00:31:19.740
